INT. TRAM DEPOT - LOST PROPERTY OFFICE - EVENING

A cage of shelves under a green-shaded lamp. Umbrellas by the hundred. Single gloves pinned to a corkboard. A brass bell on the counter.

LUCIENNE MARCHAND (30s, in a rain-spotted coat, hair damp) stands at the counter with a ticket stub clutched in her fist. FERDINAND OYELOWO (40s, the clerk, cardigan, a pencil behind his ear) sits behind the cage, writing in a ledger. MRS. ABARA (70s, regular, a tartan shopping trolley) waits by the umbrellas, politely invisible.

LUCIENNE
I lost a hat. On the number nine, yesterday, around six.

FERDINAND
Color?

LUCIENNE
Gray. Felt. A little feather in the band.

FERDINAND
Pheasant?

LUCIENNE
How did you know?

FERDINAND
It's always pheasant. Wait here, Mademoiselle Marchand.

LUCIENNE
How do you know my name?

FERDINAND
You wrote it inside the hat. I found it this morning and I've been holding it all day.

He vanishes into the shelves. Lucienne turns to Mrs. Abara, who smiles with the serenity of a woman who has seen a lot of hats.

MRS. ABARA
He's quite nice, Ferdinand.

LUCIENNE
I'm sure.

MRS. ABARA
He's been here nineteen years.

LUCIENNE
That's very long.

MRS. ABARA
He's waiting for something.

LUCIENNE
What?

MRS. ABARA
I wouldn't presume.

Ferdinand returns with the gray hat held in both hands like a small, wounded bird. He sets it on the counter. Lucienne touches the feather, turns it over, and finds her own handwriting inside the brim.

LUCIENNE
You found it.

FERDINAND
It found me. It was on the seat, face down, as if it had given up.

LUCIENNE
Thank you, Mr. Oyelowo.

FERDINAND
Ferdinand.

LUCIENNE
Ferdinand. Thank you.

FERDINAND
You'll have to sign.

He turns the ledger toward her and offers the pencil. Their fingers touch on it. Nobody moves for a moment.

MRS. ABARA
Dear me. I think I've lost my umbrella.

FERDINAND
Mrs. Abara, you came in with it.

MRS. ABARA
Did I?

FERDINAND
It's in your trolley.

MRS. ABARA
How careless of me.

Lucienne signs. She pushes the ledger back. Ferdinand reads her signature as if it were a poem.

FERDINAND
Lucienne.

LUCIENNE
Yes?

FERDINAND
I should say, I don't usually keep things. I put a hat in the box, and the box goes on the shelf. I didn't put your hat on the shelf.

LUCIENNE
Why not?

FERDINAND
I wanted to see who it belonged to.

LUCIENNE
And now you have.

FERDINAND
Yes.

Lucienne puts the hat on. She tilts it. The pheasant feather bobs. Behind the counter, Ferdinand takes the pencil from behind his ear, looks at it, and puts it back.

LUCIENNE
It suits me.

FERDINAND
It does.

LUCIENNE
Ferdinand, if I were to lose something else tomorrow--

FERDINAND
Then I would be here.

LUCIENNE
At six?

FERDINAND
At six.

She nods and turns to leave. Mrs. Abara, as she passes, taps her trolley three times with a gloved finger, as if blessing it.
